  <title>9.3.4: Completing the Square with Fractions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02375</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.3.4: Completing the Square with Fractions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9127339f-fed8-462b-949f-8203a739598c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>The solutions to this equation are \(x = \frac{3}{4}\) and \(x=\frac{1}{4}\). Show how to find those solutions by <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>. \(x^2−x+\frac{3}{16}=0\)</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}x^2-x+\frac3{16}&amp;=&amp;0\\x^2-x+\frac14&amp;=&amp;-\frac3{16}+\frac14=-\frac3{16}+\frac4{16}=\frac1{16}\\x^2-x+\frac14&amp;=&amp;\frac1{16}\\\sqrt{\left(x-\frac12\right)^2}&amp;=&amp;\sqrt{\frac1{16}}\\x-\frac12&amp;=&amp;\pm\frac14\\x-\frac12&amp;=&amp;\frac14\\x-\frac12&amp;=&amp;-\frac14\end{array}\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
